            <div xml:id="d836">

                <pb facs="0981" n="863" sameAs="frus:frus1928v02/pg863"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.6212 A/7</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Under Secretary of State of a
                        Conversation With the German Ambassador</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Von
                        Prittwitz</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1928-04-11"><hi rendition="#italic">April 11,
                        1928</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>The Ambassador came in at his own suggestion and said that his Government had
                    been considering the draft treaties of arbitration and conciliation recently
                    submitted. It was anxious to negotiate and sign these treaties as soon as
                    possible. There were, however, one or two questions of interpretation and
                    procedure under the two treaties which it would seem desirable to discuss quite
                    informally in order to make certain that there is an entire agreement on all
                    points. The Ambassador thought that these questions were easily determinable. He
                    did not wish to deal with them at all formally and, therefore, merely prepared a
                    very brief memorandum which he would <pb facs="0982" n="864" xml:id="pg864"/>
                    like to leave for our consideration. He suggested that at our convenience we
                    take the subject up again orally. Attached is his memorandum.</p>
                <p>The Ambassador also inquired whether the correspondence on the subject of the
                    treaty to outlaw war had been yet sent to Ambassador Schurman for delivery to
                    the German Government. I told him that all papers were now in the hands of our
                    Ambassador in Berlin, and that we hoped to give him final instructions to
                    deliver them to the German Government some time within the next day or two.</p>
                <closer><signed>R[<hi rendition="#small-caps">obert</hi>] E. O[<hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">lds</hi>]</signed></closer>


                <xi:include href="d836-a1.xml" xpointer="d836-a1"/>
            </div>
